digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N11" [label="(9,0,0) Currently chiral organic molecules and chiral metal complexes are tedious/costly to synthesize and often have poor thermal stability and low luminescence intensity."];
  "N14" [label="(11,0,0) Currently lead-free metal halide hybrids are a hotspot because they are low-cost, facile to synthesize, structurally diverse, and can have high PLQY."];
  "N16" [label="(13,0,0) Currently a key principle to create chirality in these hybrids is directly incorporating chiral organic cations into the metal-halide lattice to impart inherent chirality."];
  "N18" [label="(15,0,0) Currently the deformed excited state induces self-trapped excitons (STEs), enabling efficient STE emission and chiral luminescence."];
  "N20" [label="(17,0,0) Currently a 0D In–Sb chloride shows near-unity STE emission and g_lum ≈ ±1.0×10^-2."];
  "N23" [label="(20,0,0) Currently reported examples show g_lum can vary by structural type (e.g., 0D example ~±3.0×10^-4 vs 1D example ~±6.0×10^-3)."];
  "N25" [label="(22,0,0) Currently despite progress, CPL performance is not governed by clear rules and is usually achieved by trial-and-error."];
  "N26" [label="(23,0,0) Currently it is highly needed to clarify structure–chiroptical-property relationships in chiral metal halide hybrids to guide rational design of efficient CPL sources."];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) If empirical successes exist but no governing rule, then a plausible governing structural variable should be hypothesized and systematically tested to derive design guidance."];
  "N28" [label="(0,0,0) Currently candidate hypothesis: structural dimension (0D/1D/3D connectivity) is a governing variable that can tune chirality induction and the μ–m balance, thereby tuning g_lum while maintaining high PLQY."];
  "N3" [label="(2,2,0) Currently g_lum is determined by g_lum = 4 m μ cos(θ)/(m^2+μ^2), linking g_lum to the magnitudes/orientation of μ and m."];
  "N30" [label="(25,0,0) Currently rationally design 0D/1D/3D In-based hybrids by tuning size/polarization of chiral organic cations with O heteroatoms; these acceptors coordinate K+ and enhance chirality transfer."];
  "N34" [label="(28,0,0) Currently observed outcome: g_lum shows exponential growth from ~10^-3 to nearly ~10^-1 as structural dimension increases from 0D to 3D."];
  "N4" [label="(3,2,0) Currently because |μ| ≫ |m|, there is an electric–magnetic mismatch, so typical g_lum is only ~10^-4 to 10^-3."];
  "N5" [label="(0,0,0) If g_lum depends on the relative magnitudes and orientation of μ and m (per Eq.1), then improving g_lum requires modulating μ and/or m (values and/or orientation) to reduce mismatch and/or improve alignment."];
  "N9" [label="(7,0,0) Currently many CPL-active material classes exist (chiral small molecules, inorganic nanomaterials, metal complexes), showing feasibility but not necessarily practicality."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a dimension-regulation strategy for efficient CPL sources by using oxygen-bearing chiral organic cations and K+ coordination to build 0D, 1D, and 3D lead-free In-based metal-halide hybrids, testing whether increasing structural dimensionality tunes chirality induction and μ–m balance to achieve high PLQY together with large g_lum and improved CPL figure of merit.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing CPL emitters face practicality limits and prior chiral metal-halide examples show promising emission, a rational next step is to test a controllable structural variable that links chiral lattice design to μ–m balance, g_lum, PLQY, and CPL performance."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) The electric–magnetic transition-dipole mismatch makes modulation of the magnitudes and/or relative orientation of μ and m a central design issue for enhancing g_lum."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because g_lum depends on the magnitudes and relative orientation of μ and m, a hypothesis that structural dimension tunes the μ–m balance motivates testing whether dimensional regulation changes g_lum while preserving high PLQY."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Within the reported 0D/1D/3D hybrid series, the observed exponential increase of g_lum with structural dimension supports dimensional regulation as a promising design variable for improving CPL dissymmetry."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The study can develop and evaluate a dimension-regulation framework in which O-heteroatom chiral cations and K+ coordination construct 0D/1D/3D In-based hybrids to test dimension-dependent CPL performance."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Practical limitations of some existing CPL material families motivate searching for more practical CPL platforms that are stable, bright, and design-tunable."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Reported chiral lead-free metal-halide examples with high PLQY, sizable g_lum, or high FOM indicate that this material platform is viable for pursuing efficient CPL emission."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) A plausible route toward chiral luminescent metal-halide hybrids is to combine lattice chirality from chiral organic cations with excited-state deformation that can enable STE emission and chiral luminescence."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Given the need to clarify structure–chiroptical-property relationships, structural dimensionality is a motivated variable to test for tuning chirality induction, μ–m balance, g_lum, and PLQY in chiral metal halide hybrids."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The designed 0D/1D/3D In-based hybrids made by tuning O-heteroatom chiral cations and K+ coordination provide a material set for testing whether dimensional regulation can tune g_lum while maintaining high PLQY."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Structural-dimensional regulation is a plausible route to investigate for enhancing chiral induction and tuning the μ–m balance that controls g_lum."];
  "N20" -> "N27" [label="induction-case"];
